WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:01.140
JENNIFER THOMAS: So
far we've learned

00:00:01.140 --> 00:00:03.090
that a scalable app
can gracefully handle

00:00:03.090 --> 00:00:05.470
peaks and dips in traffic.

00:00:05.470 --> 00:00:07.830
But each application has
its own infrastructure

00:00:07.830 --> 00:00:09.550
and scaling requirements.

00:00:09.550 --> 00:00:12.510
Let's take a look at how Critter
Junction launched a new app

00:00:12.510 --> 00:00:14.160
and how they chose
where to run it.

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:17.618
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:21.580 --> 00:00:24.250
With more users than ever
and with their new immutable

00:00:24.250 --> 00:00:27.160
infrastructure, Critter
Junction was in a great place

00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:28.450
to launch a new app.

00:00:28.450 --> 00:00:30.520
They wanted to launch
a companion game that

00:00:30.520 --> 00:00:33.130
used the same database but
lets you build and share

00:00:33.130 --> 00:00:35.380
house layouts using
your inventory,

00:00:35.380 --> 00:00:37.690
codenamed The Layout App.

00:00:37.690 --> 00:00:39.970
With a new app launch,
scale and unpredictability

00:00:39.970 --> 00:00:41.860
is always a big challenge.

00:00:41.860 --> 00:00:44.140
You may never know
exactly how many players

00:00:44.140 --> 00:00:45.940
will show up on launch day.

00:00:45.940 --> 00:00:48.400
They needed to find a
solution on Google Cloud that

00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:51.730
could automatically
handle any amount of load.

00:00:51.730 --> 00:00:53.980
The team already knew
they wanted a layout app

00:00:53.980 --> 00:00:56.220
to scale independently
from their web servers,

00:00:56.220 --> 00:00:58.870
but they have lots of options.

00:00:58.870 --> 00:01:00.730
One solution is to
run The Layout App

00:01:00.730 --> 00:01:02.710
on a separate set of VMs.

00:01:02.710 --> 00:01:04.910
Using Google's
Global Load Balancer,

00:01:04.910 --> 00:01:08.600
you can send traffic to backend
managed instance groups.

00:01:08.600 --> 00:01:12.010
These can automatically scale
multiple identical machines

00:01:12.010 --> 00:01:14.930
based on metrics
like CPU utilization.

00:01:14.930 --> 00:01:18.160
So they could easily handle
more traffic across regions.

00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:20.080
But it turned out
The Layout App didn't

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:22.450
need to run with access
to the operating system.

00:01:22.450 --> 00:01:25.240
And the team wanted their apps
to be lightweight and portable

00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:26.440
across environments.

00:01:26.440 --> 00:01:29.410
So they decided to
containerize it.

00:01:29.410 --> 00:01:31.840
Containers are basically
an isolated package

00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:33.710
for just running an application.

00:01:33.710 --> 00:01:35.880
It's a great way to
build a cloud-native app,

00:01:35.880 --> 00:01:38.380
but you still need to serve it.

00:01:38.380 --> 00:01:40.120
Two choices for
serving containers

00:01:40.120 --> 00:01:44.620
would be managed instance groups
running container-optimized OS

00:01:44.620 --> 00:01:46.210
or Google Kubernetes Engines.

00:01:46.210 --> 00:01:49.420
Both solutions automatically
scale up as demand increases,

00:01:49.420 --> 00:01:51.580
adding new machines as
needed, and removing them

00:01:51.580 --> 00:01:53.120
when demand is low.

00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:55.660
This can increase the
availability at peak times

00:01:55.660 --> 00:01:57.633
while keeping your
costs manageable.

00:01:57.633 --> 00:01:59.050
Either would work
to run their app

00:01:59.050 --> 00:02:01.120
and meet their
unpredictable scaling needs,

00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:03.250
but the team wanted
further abstraction

00:02:03.250 --> 00:02:04.720
from the infrastructure.

00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:07.000
They decided they didn't
want upfront provisioning

00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:11.240
and wanted the ability to
scale down to zero resources.

00:02:11.240 --> 00:02:14.170
The next step was to
look at serverless tech.

00:02:14.170 --> 00:02:17.380
These technologies almost
completely abstract away

00:02:17.380 --> 00:02:19.990
the infrastructure
and orchestration.

00:02:19.990 --> 00:02:21.700
Unlike the previous
options, these

00:02:21.700 --> 00:02:23.740
handle automatic
resource scaling

00:02:23.740 --> 00:02:28.180
with virtually no configuration
or server provisioning.

00:02:28.180 --> 00:02:31.640
Serverless mainly keys in
on running your application

00:02:31.640 --> 00:02:34.150
so you can focus on your code.

00:02:34.150 --> 00:02:37.420
Google Cloud has three main
options for running serverless.

00:02:37.420 --> 00:02:40.120
First up, Cloud Functions
is great for snippets

00:02:40.120 --> 00:02:43.090
of code and small,
single-purpose applications.

00:02:43.090 --> 00:02:47.080
Functions scale by creating
new instances as demand rises.

00:02:47.080 --> 00:02:50.140
And each function
handles a single request.

00:02:50.140 --> 00:02:51.820
You can scale down
to zero and limit

00:02:51.820 --> 00:02:55.180
the maximum number of
instances for full flexibility.

00:02:55.180 --> 00:02:58.240
It's perfect for gluing together
different cloud services

00:02:58.240 --> 00:03:00.820
like calling an API
based on a schedule

00:03:00.820 --> 00:03:02.380
or sending notifications.

00:03:02.380 --> 00:03:06.520
But it was too simple for
running The Layout App, so they

00:03:06.520 --> 00:03:08.740
looked to App Engine,
which offers the ability

00:03:08.740 --> 00:03:11.800
to run containers and
custom web applications

00:03:11.800 --> 00:03:14.590
in a serverless way,
automatically creating

00:03:14.590 --> 00:03:17.980
and shutting down instances
as traffic fluctuates.

00:03:17.980 --> 00:03:20.380
You can configure the
settings for automatic scaling

00:03:20.380 --> 00:03:22.430
based on your app's needs.

00:03:22.430 --> 00:03:24.250
For example,
scaling can be based

00:03:24.250 --> 00:03:27.190
on CPU utilization,
throughput utilization,

00:03:27.190 --> 00:03:29.650
or maximum concurrent requests.

00:03:29.650 --> 00:03:31.780
This could run their
containerized app

00:03:31.780 --> 00:03:33.230
without a problem.

00:03:33.230 --> 00:03:34.990
But the team wanted
to make sure they

00:03:34.990 --> 00:03:37.780
could run their app in
multiple clouds down the road

00:03:37.780 --> 00:03:40.570
and didn't want to re-architect.

00:03:40.570 --> 00:03:42.880
Finally, there's
Cloud Run, which

00:03:42.880 --> 00:03:45.520
is built on a project
called Knative.

00:03:45.520 --> 00:03:48.460
Knative is all about serverless
running of containers

00:03:48.460 --> 00:03:50.170
on top of Kubernetes.

00:03:50.170 --> 00:03:53.200
But with Cloud Run, all
the Kubernetes action

00:03:53.200 --> 00:03:55.810
is taking care of
behind the scenes.

00:03:55.810 --> 00:03:57.970
Each deployment is
automatically scaled

00:03:57.970 --> 00:04:00.460
to the number of
instances needed to handle

00:04:00.460 --> 00:04:02.230
all incoming requests.

00:04:02.230 --> 00:04:06.460
And it can scale from zero to
your specified max instances.

00:04:06.460 --> 00:04:09.040
Because the app team
expected traffic spikes,

00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:11.530
Cloud Run let them keep
some idle instances

00:04:11.530 --> 00:04:13.240
to minimize cold starts.

00:04:13.240 --> 00:04:15.430
And if they wanted to
bring in multiple clouds,

00:04:15.430 --> 00:04:17.920
they could use the same
application and just

00:04:17.920 --> 00:04:19.450
leverage Knative.

00:04:19.450 --> 00:04:22.480
Given their needs for container
portability, fast scaling,

00:04:22.480 --> 00:04:24.550
and minimal
infrastructure overhead,

00:04:24.550 --> 00:04:26.530
the team decided on
Cloud Run and was

00:04:26.530 --> 00:04:29.530
able to launch faster than
they thought possible.

00:04:29.530 --> 00:04:32.320
While each option has
auto-scaling capabilities built

00:04:32.320 --> 00:04:34.810
in, you can decide
which of these solutions

00:04:34.810 --> 00:04:37.300
works best for the
requirements of your app.

00:04:37.300 --> 00:04:40.540
Plus, you can use different
solutions for different apps,

00:04:40.540 --> 00:04:43.570
giving you the power
to choose and scale.

00:04:43.570 --> 00:04:46.210
Stay tuned to find out what's
in store for Critter Junction.

00:04:46.210 --> 00:04:48.370
And remember, always
be architecting.

00:04:48.370 --> 00:04:52.920
[MUSIC PLAYING]

